--TEST--
Core\Validate::isPrintable asks whether any character is in Unicode general category Cc, where ctype_print asks whether every byte is in the C locale's 0x20 to 0x7E band — so `café`, a non-breaking space and a bidi override are printable here and are not there, the C1 controls are refused by both for different reasons, and the empty string is printable here where every ctype_* answers false
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
The two agree over the whole 7-bit band — `validate-is-ascii-and-is-printable-match-a-byte-band-probe-and-ctype_print-over-the-ascii-band` sweeps all 128 code points and pins that — and they part everywhere else, because they are not asking the same question: `ctype_print` is a byte test under a process-global locale, while this member decodes the subject and asks Unicode's own question, `char::is_control` (`validate.rs:601`), which is exactly general category `Cc`. Three consequences follow, and each is a row below. Text that is printable in any language but English is printable here and is not in PHP, which is the whole reason the member exists at all — spec § 1 keeps `Core\Validate::isAscii` and `isPrintable` while dropping the rest of `ctype_*` precisely because importing ASCII-only semantics into a type `rule:types/bytes` guarantees is UTF-8 is the mistake that ADR exists to prevent, and `café` being unprintable is that mistake in one word. The C1 controls `U+0085` and `U+009F` are refused by both, which looks like agreement and is not: PHP refuses them for being bytes outside its band, and this refuses them for being `Cc`, so a non-breaking space at `U+00A0` — one code point later, and the first that is not `Cc` — parts them again. And the empty string is `true` here where every `ctype_*` member documents `false` for it, because *no character is a control* is vacuously satisfied, which is the same reading `Core\Arr::all` gives an empty array. Nothing here is a spoofing check in either language: a bidi override and a zero-width joiner are printable, and refusing them under this name would be the false confidence `rule:security/tainted-qualifier` exists to prevent — `validate.rs`'s module docs are the home of that argument.
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Show {
    public static function yn(bool $b): string {
        if ($b) {
            return "y";
        }
        return "n";
    }
}

// Vacuously true: there is no character in the subject, so there is none in
// `Cc`. `ctype_print('')` is documented `false`.
echo "empty:", Show::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable("")), "\n";

// Printable text that is not ASCII — the band `ctype_print` refuses wholesale
// because every byte of it is above `0x7F`.
echo "text:", Show::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable("café")),
     Show::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable("日本語")),
     Show::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable("→")), "\n";

// Three code points that are not `Cc` and are not printable in any ordinary
// sense either: a non-breaking space (`Zs`), a zero-width space and a
// right-to-left override (both `Cf`). All three are printable here, and the
// module docs own why refusing them under this name would be a spoofing check
// wearing a validator's name.
echo "format:", Show::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable(Core\Str::fromCodePoint(160 as uint))),
     Show::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable(Core\Str::fromCodePoint(8203 as uint))),
     Show::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable(Core\Str::fromCodePoint(8238 as uint))), "\n";

// The C1 controls, which are `Cc` and are above the band: refused here for the
// reason the member states, and by `ctype_print` for a reason that happens to
// coincide. One code point past the second of them the two part again, which
// is the `160` row above.
echo "c1:", Show::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable(Core\Str::fromCodePoint(133 as uint))),
     Show::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable(Core\Str::fromCodePoint(159 as uint))),
     Show::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable("a" . Core\Str::fromCodePoint(133 as uint) . "b")), "\n";
--EXPECT--
empty:y
text:yyy
format:yyy
c1:nnn
